1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,793
A man is walking downhill through Jerusalem with mud on his eyes. He cannot see. He
has never seen.

2
00:00:08,843 --> 00:00:21,470
And yet a complete stranger has just given him shocking news about his blindness,
and told him to go and wash in a pool at the bottom of the city. And. He goes.

3
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:36,327
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church. I am the narrator.

4
00:00:36,377 --> 00:00:45,214
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

5
00:00:45,264 --> 00:00:48,250
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

6
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:54,736
First, where we are. Jesus is in Jerusalem.

7
00:00:54,786 --> 00:01:05,263
The Feast of Tabernacles has just ended, and at the close of the previous chapter
the crowd in the Temple picked up stones to throw at Him. He slipped away.

8
00:01:05,313 --> 00:01:15,790
Now, walking on, He sees a man who has been blind from birth, sitting and begging.
It is the Sabbath. John chapter 9, verses 1 to 5.

9
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:32,801
[Reader] Now as Jesus was passing by, He saw a man blind from birth, and His
disciples asked Him, “Rabbi, who sinned, this man or his parents, that he was born
blind?”

10
00:01:32,851 --> 00:01:42,106
[Reader] Jesus answered, “Neither this man nor his parents sinned, but this happened
so that the works of God would be displayed in him.

11
00:01:42,156 --> 00:01:54,110
[Reader] While it is daytime, we must do the works of Him who sent Me. Night is
coming, when no one can work. While I am in the world, I am the light of the world.”

12
00:01:55,593 --> 00:02:05,123
Notice who speaks first. Not the blind man. The disciples, about him, in front of
him, as a question in theology.

13
00:02:05,173 --> 00:02:15,893
Alfred Edersheim, writing in 1883, says this was a common question in that day:
rabbis meeting such a person would ask by what sin the affliction had come,

14
00:02:15,943 --> 00:02:20,409
and many believed the faults of parents showed in their children.

15
00:02:20,459 --> 00:02:30,665
Jesus cuts across both ideas with one sentence. A word about the voice you are about
to hear. Brian decided he was too shy to record his own thoughts.

16
00:02:30,715 --> 00:02:42,030
We suspect he was being a scaredy cat. So, against our better judgment, we let him
pick his own voice. Whenever you hear it, from here to the end, these are his words.

17
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:55,715
[Brian] This one makes me cry because of what Jesus first says. Neither this man nor
his parents sinned, but this happened so that the works of God would be displayed in
him.

18
00:02:55,765 --> 00:03:04,261
[Brian] That is what I pray for daily: that God uses my blindness for His glory in
the things I do, the things I say, and the way I treat others,

19
00:03:04,311 --> 00:03:10,810
[Brian] especially those who need help the most and who have, according to many,
absolutely nothing to give back.

20
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,590
John chapter 9, verses 6 to 7.

21
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:25,159
[Reader] When Jesus had said this, He spit on the ground, made some mud, and applied
it to the man’s eyes.

22
00:03:25,209 --> 00:03:35,290
[Reader] Then He told him, “Go, wash in the Pool of Siloam” (which means “Sent”). So
the man went and washed, and came back seeing.

23
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:39,961
Saliva and mud.

24
00:03:40,011 --> 00:03:45,047
Edersheim notes that saliva was commonly thought to help diseases of the eye,

25
00:03:45,097 --> 00:03:52,379
and that making clay and anointing on the Sabbath were among the things the
religious teachers counted as work.

26
00:03:52,429 --> 00:04:00,200
Hold on to that. It is why the day matters so much in the argument to come. And the
pool.

27
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:05,803
Siloam lies at the south end of Jerusalem, the lowest point of the ancient city.

28
00:04:05,853 --> 00:04:13,063
Josephus, who knew the city before it fell, called it a fountain with sweet water in
great plenty.

29
00:04:13,113 --> 00:04:21,342
Its water came from the Gihon spring through a tunnel King Hezekiah cut so that a
besieging army could not reach the spring.

30
00:04:21,392 --> 00:04:30,697
The pool itself was lost for nineteen centuries, until 2004, when workers repairing
a sewer uncovered stone steps.

31
00:04:30,747 --> 00:04:43,353
Archaeologists found a large stone-lined basin with steps on at least three sides,
built in sets of five with landings between. How far did he walk?

32
00:04:43,403 --> 00:04:51,884
From the Temple Mount down to the pool is about six hundred metres in a straight
line, and more than a hundred metres downhill,

33
00:04:51,934 --> 00:04:56,519
through a valley Josephus describes with steep slopes on either side.

34
00:04:56,569 --> 00:05:04,885
Excavators have found a stepped street running down that valley to the pool. And at
the end, a staircase down to the water.

35
00:05:04,935 --> 00:05:10,972
Scripture does not tell us how he felt on that walk, or whether anyone helped him.

36
00:05:11,022 --> 00:05:19,533
It tells us he had just heard, for the first time in his life, a teacher say that
his blindness was not a punishment.

37
00:05:19,583 --> 00:05:26,230
So we imagine a man who had been given a reason to hope. This is a scene, not
Scripture.

38
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:43,049
[Man] Mud. Cool and wet and heavy on my eyes. His thumbs pressed it in. Go to
Siloam, he said. Wash. I have sat at the top of this road all my life.

39
00:05:43,099 --> 00:05:50,651
[Man] I know it by the slope under my feet, by the street narrowing, by the bread
ovens on the left and the sound of a side street opening on the right.

40
00:05:50,701 --> 00:05:52,730
[Man] I have never been to the bottom.

41
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:58,350
[Neighbour] Friend, your face. Where are you going like that, against all these
people?

42
00:05:59,059 --> 00:06:05,010
[Man] Down to Siloam, to wash. A man put this on my eyes. They call him Jesus.

43
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:11,550
[Neighbour] Down? With the whole city coming up for the Sabbath? Do you know the
way, and the steps at the bottom?

44
00:06:12,021 --> 00:06:20,701
[Man] I know the way I got here. People have told me all my life that my eyes were a
punishment, mine or my parents'. He said no.

45
00:06:20,751 --> 00:06:29,690
[Man] He said it was so God could do something in me. I do not know what. But I am
going to find out, and I am going to go the way he said.

46
00:06:31,169 --> 00:06:39,230
[Helper] I am going down to wash before I go up. Take my arm. The street steps down
in a little while, and I will tell you when.

47
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:41,130
[Man] Thank you. Thank you.

48
00:06:44,326 --> 00:06:56,941
[Man] His arm is steady. The crowd parts around us, voices going up, ours going
down. The paving changes under my sandals, smooth stone, then a step down. Another.

49
00:06:56,991 --> 00:07:03,431
[Man] The wall on my right gives way to open air. Somewhere below, water being
poured. And the air.

50
00:07:03,481 --> 00:07:12,963
[Man] Cool air rising up the street to meet us, wet, with the smell of stone that is
always in shadow. I have felt that at the Temple when the water was carried up.

51
00:07:13,013 --> 00:07:14,450
[Man] Now I am walking into it.

52
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:21,970
[Helper] Stairs here. The landing is wide. Then more stairs, and the water.

53
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:30,250
[Man] My foot finds the edge of the stone. The water is cold on my hands. I kneel
beside him, and I wash.

54
00:07:30,423 --> 00:07:37,610
What happened next, Scripture gives in three words. He came back seeing.

55
00:07:43,782 --> 00:07:52,945
[Brian] One of my first true walk-by-faith moments came during rehabilitation
training at the Hines VA hospital outside Chicago. At the end of training I did a
drop-off test.

56
00:07:52,995 --> 00:08:00,164
[Brian] I was let out of a car in a suburban business district with the task of
finding a grocery store about five blocks away, on my own.

57
00:08:00,214 --> 00:08:07,489
[Brian] Unless I got into serious physical danger, I was not to be helped. I had to
trust my new skills to make that trip without any sight.

58
00:08:07,539 --> 00:08:11,735
[Brian] It was terrifying, exhilarating and liberating all at once.

59
00:08:11,785 --> 00:08:20,650
[Brian] I think of the man born blind making his way to the Pool of Siloam, not
using a fancy iPhone while using the latest in cane technology, but by faith.

60
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:27,750
But it never goes easy, does it? John chapter 9, verses 8 to 12.

61
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:39,188
[Reader] At this, his neighbors and those who had formerly seen him begging began to
ask, “Isn’t this the man who used to sit and beg?”

62
00:08:39,238 --> 00:08:52,227
[Reader] Some claimed that he was, but others said, “No, he just looks like him.”
But the man kept saying, “I am the one.” “How then were your eyes opened?” they
asked.

63
00:08:52,277 --> 00:09:04,544
[Reader] He answered, “The man they call Jesus made some mud and anointed my eyes,
and He told me to go to Siloam and wash. So I went and washed and received my
sight.”

64
00:09:04,594 --> 00:09:08,930
[Reader] “Where is He?” they asked. “I do not know,” he answered.

65
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:19,068
[Brian] He was blind from birth and had never had sight. From a physiological
standpoint his visual cortex would never have developed.

66
00:09:19,118 --> 00:09:28,399
[Brian] It takes a child about six years of seeing to learn to tell faces apart. He
washed the mud off and received sight. Jesus did not only heal the eyes.

67
00:09:28,449 --> 00:09:32,070
[Brian] He must have given the man a way to process what his eyes were now sending
to his brain.

68
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:38,750
John chapter 9, verses 13 to 17.

69
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:50,311
[Reader] They brought to the Pharisees the man who had been blind. Now the day on
which Jesus had made the mud and opened his eyes was a Sabbath.

70
00:09:50,361 --> 00:10:01,381
[Reader] So the Pharisees also asked him how he had received his sight. The man
answered, “He put mud on my eyes, and I washed, and now I can see.”

71
00:10:01,431 --> 00:10:14,313
[Reader] Because of this, some of the Pharisees said, “This man is not from God, for
He does not keep the Sabbath.” But others said, “How can a sinful man perform such
signs?”

72
00:10:14,363 --> 00:10:25,211
[Reader] And there was division among them. So once again they asked the man who had
been blind, “What do you say about Him, since it was your eyes He opened?”

73
00:10:25,261 --> 00:10:27,290
[Reader] “He is a prophet,” the man replied.

74
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:36,010
[Brian] The Pharisees went straight to whether the healing was lawful, not whether
it had happened.

75
00:10:37,184 --> 00:10:42,690
Then they send for his parents. John chapter 9, verses 18 to 23.

76
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:56,062
[Reader] The Jews still did not believe that the man had been blind and had received
his sight until they summoned his parents and asked, “Is this your son,

77
00:10:56,112 --> 00:10:58,566
[Reader] the one you say was born blind?

78
00:10:58,616 --> 00:11:07,693
[Reader] So how is it that he can now see?” His parents answered, “We know he is our
son, and we know he was born blind.

79
00:11:07,743 --> 00:11:17,253
[Reader] But how he can now see or who opened his eyes, we do not know. Ask him. He
is old enough to speak for himself.”

80
00:11:17,303 --> 00:11:20,926
[Reader] His parents said this because they were afraid of the Jews.

81
00:11:20,976 --> 00:11:31,715
[Reader] For the Jews had already determined that anyone who confessed Jesus as the
Christ would be put out of the synagogue. That was why his parents said, “He is old
enough.

82
00:11:31,765 --> 00:11:32,270
[Reader] Ask him.”

83
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:37,450
John chapter 9, verses 24 to 34.

84
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:48,884
[Reader] So a second time they called for the man who had been blind and said, “Give
glory to God! We know that this man is a sinner.”

85
00:11:48,934 --> 00:12:01,062
[Reader] He answered, “Whether He is a sinner I do not know. There is one thing I do
know: I was blind, but now I see!” “What did He do to you?” they asked.

86
00:12:01,112 --> 00:12:12,325
[Reader] “How did He open your eyes?” He replied, “I already told you, and you did
not listen. Why do you want to hear it again? Do you also want to become His
disciples?”

87
00:12:12,375 --> 00:12:19,524
[Reader] Then they heaped insults on him and said, “You are His disciple; we are
disciples of Moses.

88
00:12:19,574 --> 00:12:27,741
[Reader] We know that God spoke to Moses, but we do not know where this man is
from.” “That is remarkable indeed!” the man said.

89
00:12:27,791 --> 00:12:32,481
[Reader] “You do not know where He is from, and yet He opened my eyes.

90
00:12:32,531 --> 00:12:40,070
[Reader] We know that God does not listen to sinners, but He does listen to the one
who worships Him and does His will.

91
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:49,498
[Reader] Never before has anyone heard of opening the eyes of a man born blind. If
this man were not from God, He could do no such thing.”

92
00:12:49,548 --> 00:12:55,990
[Reader] They replied, “You were born in utter sin, and you are instructing us?” And
they threw him out.

93
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,122
[Brian] The way he argues with the Pharisees is simply amazing.

94
00:13:05,172 --> 00:13:15,191
[Brian] He would never have had access to Scripture or learning, and yet with a
plain explanation he cuts through the arguments of the experts, in Jerusalem, and
wins.

95
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:20,550
[Brian] That he would even speak back to a Pharisee is remarkable. His parents were
obviously terrified.

96
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:28,747
A word on begging, since this man had begged all his life.

97
00:13:28,797 --> 00:13:39,949
Edersheim says blind beggars were a familiar sight at the Temple entrance, that the
blind were held to be specially entitled to charity, and that a common plea was,

98
00:13:39,999 --> 00:13:41,110
gain merit by me.

99
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:49,590
[Brian] How humbling, to call out in effect: I have so little to offer you that your
kindness to me will gain you righteousness.

100
00:13:52,640 --> 00:14:02,750
They throw him out. And then the one sentence that holds the whole chapter. John
chapter 9, verses 35 to 38.

101
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:14,066
[Reader] When Jesus heard that they had thrown him out, He found the man and said,
“Do you believe in the Son of Man?” “Who is He, Sir?” he replied.

102
00:14:14,116 --> 00:14:25,054
[Reader] “Tell me so that I may believe in Him.” “You have already seen Him,” Jesus
answered. “He is the One speaking with you.” “Lord, I believe,” he said.

103
00:14:25,104 --> 00:14:26,730
[Reader] And he worshiped Jesus.

104
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:36,270
He had heard that voice once before, over the mud. He had never seen the face. Now
he sees it.

105
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:45,156
[Brian] It is hard to express what that man must have felt when he finally
recognised Jesus, after Jesus sought him out.

106
00:14:45,206 --> 00:14:53,990
[Brian] I am so grateful to Jesus for what He did for that one blind man. Some day,
I will see again, and I can't wait for my moment of seeing Jesus for the first time
too.

107
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:00,910
John chapter 9, verses 39 to 41.

108
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:11,661
[Reader] Then Jesus declared, “For judgment I have come into this world, so that the
blind may see and those who see may become blind.”

109
00:15:11,711 --> 00:15:25,190
[Reader] Some of the Pharisees who were with Him heard this, and they asked Him,
“Are we blind too?” “If you were blind,” Jesus replied, “you would not be guilty of
sin.

110
00:15:25,240 --> 00:15:29,210
[Reader] But since you claim you can see, your guilt remains.”

111
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:43,830
Notice where the word blind lands at the end of the chapter. Not on the man who was
born blind. On the men who could see.

112
00:15:47,253 --> 00:15:55,834
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

113
00:15:55,884 --> 00:16:10,264
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

114
00:16:10,314 --> 00:16:14,250
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

